WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.000
-Our next guest
are world-famous magicians

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.534
and the longest-running
headliners

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.701
in Las Vegas history.
-History!

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:09.467
-History.
Their CW show,

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:13.067
"Penn and Teller: Fool Us,"
airs Friday nights at 8:00 p.m.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.234
Please welcome Penn and Teller!

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:18.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.067
Good to see you.
-Gotta say,

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.834
I'm so glad you said,
"The longest running show

00:00:22.834 --> 00:00:24.534
in the history of Las Vegas."

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:26.634
-In the history of Las Vegas.
-That way, we're competing

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:29.200
with Sinatra and Elvis
and winning.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.367
-Is that right?
-We've run longer

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.033
than anyone ever in Las Vegas.

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.267
-You're at the Rio, right?

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:34.534
-Yeah, yeah.
-For over 20 years.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.901
-Over 20 years.
Headliners all the time, yep.

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:38.701
-That's un--
-In the conveniently named

00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:40.667
Penn and Teller Theater.
-Wow!

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:43.200
That's well-deserved, buddy.
-What are the chances of that?

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.334
-Since we've had you on,
you've always been there.

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:47.167
You were even there before,
by the way, in Vegas.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:48.634
You were there
for 10 years before that.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:50.467
-Yeah, there's --
we've been there for 30 years.

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:51.634
-Another record-breaking thing.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.834
-Your CW show,
"Penn and Teller: Fool Us,"

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:55.300
was just renewed
for a 10th season,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.267
making it the longest-running
magic show in TV history.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.634
-There you go!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:02.067
There you go.
-History yet again.

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:04.801
-History, history.
-Las Vegas history, TV history.

00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:06.467
Sorry. I really put
some oomph in there.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:09.200
-The downside of that means
that we have written, like,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.767
I think, 150 magic tricks
to close "Fool Us."

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:15.501
We've written a lot of magic.

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:16.767
-Do people come up to you
all the time

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:18.567
and want to show you tricks?
-Well, yeah.

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.934
Well, that's
how "Fool Us" started, you know?

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:23.133
Vegas is the magic capital
of the world.

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.467
Magicians would come to Vegas,
come to our show.

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:28.868
They'd go backstage and say,
"Hey, have you seen this?"

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:30.167
And they would just
look at Teller,

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.133
and if he kind of went, "Oh,"

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.501
they'd go, "Oh, great!
We fooled them!"

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:35.000
And if we went,
"Mm, that's okay,"

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.167
they would go, "No, no."

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:38.367
So we kind of liked
that backstage thing,

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.934
and that's what
"Fool Us" grew out of.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:41.267
-Yeah.
And then how do you --

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.000
but then how do you keep
coming up with new tricks?

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.667
Once you've seen it all,
you go, "Oh."

00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:46.467
-What we've doing lately is,
we'll go

00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:48.367
and take really old tricks,
like wicked old,

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:49.868
like public domain...
-Okay.

00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:52.868
-...and then working with those
until they turn into something

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.000
we think is --
We're gonna show you tonight

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.868
the trick we started with,
we'll teach you how to do that,

00:01:57.868 --> 00:01:59.467
then we'll show you the trick
we ended up with, okay?

00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:00.834
-That's what I'm talking about.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:03.334
-So the trick we started with...
-Okay.

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:06.767
-...is a trick called the
doughnut on the ribbon trick,

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:09.834
which is a rather prosaic name,
but I didn't name it.

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:11.934
You have your friend just --
You can come over here.

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:14.634
Just thread a doughnut
onto a ribbon. Okay?

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:15.734
-Okay.
-Then you have them

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.667
hold both ends.
You can hold both ends

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:20.000
like, hold the ribbon
where I am,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.300
and then hold it over here.
Right?

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.801
Then you talk about
how doughnuts have integrity,

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:26.300
not just ethically and morally
but also structurally.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.701
-Yeah, of course.
-You can't get

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:29.734
the doughnut off there.
Well, you're holding both ends.

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:32.200
Then you just put
a napkin over like this,

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.701
and then, lo and behold,
magically.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:39.067
You pull the doughnut
off the ribbon.

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:41.567
And, see, your friend
can examine the doughnut.

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:43.601
It's totally legit.
It's righteous.

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:44.934
They can examine the ribbon,

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.934
which is totally legit,
righteous,

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:48.667
Nothing fishy about the props.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:51.434
And they can also
examine the napkin,

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:54.234
which has nothing unusual
about it whatsoever.

00:02:54.234 --> 00:02:55.434
-Now...
-Wow.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:58.067
-...you might want
to do that at home.

00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:00.267
And if you want to,
it's really simple.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.734
We're gonna
teach you the trick.

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:04.834
There's no magic moves at all.
First part, totally legit.

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:08.567
You just thread the doughnut
onto the ribbon. Okay?

00:03:08.567 --> 00:03:10.534
And then you hold both ends
like you did before.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:12.667
Now watch this.
No magic moves, nothing.

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:15.934
While you're talking
about integrity, you cop a dupe.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:17.834
So you have a duplicate doughnut
over here.

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:19.734
It's under the napkin.
No magic moves.

00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:20.934
-Okay.
-Then you come over here,

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:21.934
and then when you're
under the napkin,

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.334
we showed you
another napkin.

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:26.434
Just rip it apart,
and you hand them the duplicate.

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:27.601
-Okay.
-And then while they're

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:30.334
examining this,
you just eat the evidence.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:33.000
[ Laughter ]
So you end up totally clean.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.567
-Yeah, brilliantly done.
-So, we looked at this trick,

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:39.467
and we thought, "What could we
do to make this a new trick?"

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:41.300
And we thought the most
important part of this

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.801
is the shape of the doughnut.
So what else has that shape?

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:48.200
And the answer comes back,
how about an inner tube?

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.501
Essentially the same shape
as a doughnut,

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:52.701
also has the integrity.
Okay?

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:55.000
Now we'll have
Nathan Santucci come out here,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.300
and we're gonna thread
the inner tube onto a rope.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:04.501
Now, just you can grab that side
over there that Nathan has.

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.667
Okay?
It's the same trick, right?

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:07.968
-I feel bad for Teller, but,
yeah.

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:09.501
-You got one end,
I get the other end, right?

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.000
And then we've got
an inner tube on there.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.667
The audience understands
everything about that, right?

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.567
-Yeah.
-And then Teller comes out

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.801
with the same napkin.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.968
-Now you know
how the trick is done.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:23.334
-Jimmy's got one end.
I've got the other end.

00:04:23.334 --> 00:04:24.734
Okay?
You understand?

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:27.968
It's topologically impossible
to get that off there.

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:29.367
-Yeah.
-Same trick as the doughnut.

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:30.634
Same trick as the doughnut?
-Yes.

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:32.801
-We're holding both ends.
You're not letting go.

00:04:32.801 --> 00:04:34.000
[ Pop! ]
Nothing fishy whatsoever.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.701
-The rope is entirely intact.
-Unbelievable.

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:40.868
And then, you can also
examine the inner tube,

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.400
and it's totally intact.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.133
And that is --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.133 --> 00:04:47.901
That is the Penn and Teller
Inner Tube Trick!

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:50.200
-Come on.
That's how you do it!

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.634
Penn and Teller, everybody!
-That's how it's done!

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:55.033
-"Penn and Teller: Fool Us"

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:57.901
airs Fridays at 8:00 p.m.
on The CW.

00:04:57.901 --> 00:04:59.200
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.467
Stick around, everybody.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:01.801
Want me to give you
the Heimlich?

